{"i": 0, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"lost‑wax sprue tree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Sprue-Tree's Lament\n\n'Twas sprue, and the waxy wicks\nDid gangle and gleam in the vat.\nAll glimmery were the sprue-sticks,\nAnd the wax-worms did skitter and plat.\n\n\"Beware the Sprue-Tree, my son!\nThe branches that twist, the roots that entwine!\nBeware the mold-slug, and shun\nThe jabbering of the Wax-Mine!\"\n\nHe took his sprue-knife in hand;\nLong time the Sprue-Tree foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the slag-stand,\nAnd pondered in thoughtful thought.\n\nAnd, as in molten thought he stood,\nThe Sprue-Tree, with eyes of fire,\nCame whiffling through the sprue-wood,\nAnd goggled with dire desire!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe sprue-knife went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its screw\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Sprue-Tree?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas sprue, and the waxy wicks\nDid gangle and gleam in the vat.\nAll glimmery were the sprue-sticks,\nAnd the wax-worms did skitter and plat.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 1, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"kintsugi seam of gold\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "The Auric Seam of Kintsugi\n\n'Twas brillig, and the china-ware\nDid fracture in the scullery,\nAll shardy were the cups, and there\nDid gleam the shards of pottery.\n\n\"Beware the Kintsug, my son!\nThe joins that bind, the flaws that shine!\nBeware the Crumblesome, and shun\nThe shattering, the fine-spun line!\"\n\nHe took his trusty brush in hand;\nLong time the broken foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the mending stand,\nAnd stood a-mending, lost in thought.\n\nAnd, as in polished thought he stood,\nThe Kintsug, with its golden gleam,\nCame mending through the broken wood,\nAnd bubbled, like a gilded stream!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe brush went glinting, mending fast!\nIt left it bright, and golden-new,\nAnd left it mended, built to last.\n\n\"And hast thou joined the Kintsug then?\nCome to my arms, my mending boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the china-ware\nDid fracture in the scullery,\nAll shardy were the cups, and there\nDid gleam the shards of pottery.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 2, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"alpenglow on scree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Scree-Glow**\n\n'Twas alpenglow, and the slippy scree\nDid tumble and crumble with mizzling sound;\nAll glidgy were the rocks, and he\nDid scrabble and stumble around.\n\n\"Beware the scree, my son! The rocks that bite,\nThe slip-foot's bane, the chasm's maw!\nBeware the crumbleshanks, and shun\nThe frumious rock-fall's law!\"\n\nHe took his ice-axe in hand;\nLong time the mizzling foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the craggy stand,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in thought he stood, the Scree-Glow, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the mizzle-wood, and burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe ice-axe went with a snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its hue\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Scree-Glow?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas alpenglow, and the slippy scree\nDid tumble and crumble with mizzling sound;\nAll glidgy were the rocks, and he\nDid scrabble and stumble around.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 3, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"plane iron camber\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Flibber-Gible and the Camber-Plane**\n\n'Twas brillig, and the camber-plane did gyre and gimble in the air;\nAll mimsy were the gyro-gangs, and the flibber-gible whiffling there.\n\n\"Beware the Flibber-Gible, my son! The jaws that bite, the claws that snatch!\nBeware the camber-plane, and shun the frumious Bandersnatch!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand; long time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree, and stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood, the Flibber-Gible, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood, and burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through the vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head he went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Flibber-Gible? Come to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\" He chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the camber-plane did gyre and gimble in the air;\nAll mimsy were the gyro-gangs, and the flibber-gible whiffling there.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 4, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"badger knot bloom\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Badgerknot's Bloom\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.\n\n\"Beware the Badgerknot, my son!\nThe jaws that munch, the claws that catch!\nBeware the Jujubird, and shun\nThe frumious Bumblepatch!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand:\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Badgerknot, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffing through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Badgerknot?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 5, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"summit prayer flag\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "The Prayer Flag's Flummox\n\n'Twas wubby, and the summit-prags\nDid gyre and gimble in the wind;\nAll flimsy were the banner-rags,\nAnd the chillupers outgrinn'd.\n\nBeware the Prayer-Flag, child, beware!\nThe flapping folds, the whisper-shout!\nBeware the Summit-Prag, and shun\nThe frumious blizzard's rout.\n\nHe took his penknife in his hand:\nLong time the mountain-foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood a while in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Prayer-Flag, with its eyes aflame,\nCame whiffling through the blizzard wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe penknife went, and then he stood\nBeside the banner, sliced in two;\nHe sliced it as he could.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Prayer-Flag bold?\nBring forth the head, and give it here!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his cheer.\n\n'Twas wubby, and the summit-prags\nDid gyre and gimble in the wind;\nAll flimsy were the banner-rags,\nAnd the chillupers outgrinn'd.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 6, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"slipjoint walk and talk\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Slipjoint Walk and Talk**\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slipjoints did\nGo walkandtalk-ing through the glade;\nAll mimsy were the grips, unbid,\nAnd the blades were sharp and unafraid.\n\n\"Beware the Fliggleflop, my son!\nThe jaws that snap, the cuts that catch!\nBeware the Flimflam bird, and shun\nThe frumious Grumblegatch!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal knife in hand:\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Fliggleflop, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Fliggleflop?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slipjoints did\nGo walkandtalk-ing through the glade;\nAll mimsy were the grips, unbid,\nAnd the blades were sharp and unafraid.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 7, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"rain on a tin roof\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "The Plinkering Roonfoof \n\n'Twas splishy, and the drickle drops\nDid plinkle on the tinny thatch;\nAll mimbling were the window-slops,\nAnd the wind a-clitter-clatch.\n\n\"Beware the Plinkering Roonfoof, my son!\nThe grumble-rumble, and the drip-drap sound!\nBeware the puddly, slizzling fun,\nAnd shun the watery, soppy ground!\"\n\nHe took his wellington and galoshes in hand;\nLong time the sploshing foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the window-stand,\nAnd in deep thought he burbledaught.\n\nAnd as in burbly thought he stood,\nThe Plinkering Roonfoof, with eyes aflame,\nCame whizzing through the misty wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe tinny roof did jingle-jank!\nHe left it dripping, wet and blue,\nAnd burbled as it sank.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Plinkering Roonfoof?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas splishy, and the drickle drops\nDid plinkle on the tinny thatch;\nAll mimbling were the window-slops,\nAnd the wind a-clitter-clatch.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 8, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"makeready under tympan\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Makeready Under Tympan\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.\n\n\"Beware the Makeready, my son!\nThe jaws that bite, the claws that catch!\nBeware the Tympan-bird, and shun\nThe frumious Bandersnatch!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand:\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Makeready, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Makeready?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 9, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"modular synth patch spaghetti\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Oscillator's Lament**\n\n'Twas patch-time, and the modules bright\nDid bleep and buzz with sonic might;\nAll messy-squiggled, long and looped,\nThe spaghetti, oh, how it blooped!\n\nBeware the VCA, my son,\nThe envelope, the CV's run!\nBeware the Filter, sharp and deep,\nAnd shun the LFO's restless leap!\n\nHe took his cables, green and red;\nLong time the modulation he had fed.\nThen stood he by the synth-rack tall,\nAnd waited for the audio's call.\n\nAnd, as in thought he stood and hummed,\nThe patch-spaghetti, wild and numbed,\nCame wending through the jacks and slots,\nAnd gurgled, \"Oombah! Bloob-bloob-blots!\"\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe jack-points flickered, bright and new!\nHe chopped the gain, and dimmed the light,\nThe patch-spaghetti, left and right!\n\n\"And hast thou tamed the tangled dread?\nThe patch-spaghetti, cleanly bred?\nO joyous day! Calloo! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his sweet array.\n\n'Twas patch-time, and the modules bright\nDid bleep and buzz with sonic might;\nAll neatly-ordered, short and looped,\nThe spaghetti now, no longer blooped!", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
